rait values sourced)","SoilHealth: insufficient sourced data (6 of 20 trait values sourced)"],"why_suggested":"The field is bare about 57% of the year under corn-soybean. Cereal rye seeded right after harvest (S05) covers the soil through winter and spring, when much of the county's heavy rain falls.","evidence":[{"claim":"Share of the grain crops' critical periods falling in months the model found costly","data_source_id":"D2;D1;S01","metric":"stress exposure (model-weighted)","value":0.581},{"claim":"Share of the year with bare soil","data_source_id":"S01;S04;S05","metric":"bare fraction","value":0.018},{"claim":"Bare soil during months that bring Bremer County's heavy-rain days","data_source_id":"D2","metric":"erosion exposure (share of heavy-rain days on bare soil)","value":0.024}],"benefits":["Bare-soil share falls from 57% to 2% of the year.","Rye produces 3,000-10,000 lb/acre of dry matter (SARE, S06) and holds soil and nutrients (S05).","Same cash crops, so no new grain markets are needed."],"information_gaps":["Field-level soil, drainage and slope: the tool uses county-scale data only.","Local markets and prices (see ISU Ag Decision Maker crop budgets, S10).","Your equipment, labour and custom-hire options.","Yield response of crops the model does not cover (oats, red clover, cereal rye)."],"risks_needing_local_review":["Rye decomposition ties up soil nitrogen for the next corn crop, more if rye has started jointing; terminate at least 10-14 days before corn planting (ISU, S04).","Possible corn yield drag after rye; Iowa survey data show smaller drag for experienced users (S12).","Seeding right after harvest and spring termination timing both depend on the season (S04; S05).","Without cost-share or grazing, cover crops returned about -$38 to -$49 per acre in an Iowa survey (S12); check local cost-share programs."],"modeled_years":2,"not_modeled_years":0,"cycle_years":2,"confidence_note":"2 of 2 rotation years are modelled; 13 of 30 trait values sourced, 30 